Done. Implemented `store::rename` in `rust/src/store.rs` (the only file changed; scratch verification test removed).

**What it does** — mirrors the existing `save`/`load_all` format (`<id>\n---\n<title>\n---\n<pinned>\n---\n<body>`):
1. Reads the old file `<old_id>.note`; if it's missing or malformed (not 4 fields), returns an `Err`.
2. Reconstructs the content with the new id and new title, reusing the old body (`parts[3]`) and re-deriving the pinned flag from `parts[2]` — so body, tags (derived from body), and pinned state are untouched.
3. Writes `<new_id>.note`, then removes `<old_id>.note` (skipped when `old_id == new_id`).

**Verified** (cargo build + `cargo test`, all green):
- New id/title, unchanged body/tags/pinned; exactly one note returned by `load_all`; old file gone; no `old-id` string leaking into the new file.
- Pinned state preserved across a rename.
- Missing old id returns an error.

Edge cases handled: `old_id == new_id` (no stale file left), missing/malformed source returns `Err`, and the new file is written before the old one is deleted so a failure mid-rename doesn't destroy the note.


